## Hypothesis (data-grounded answer)

**The Black-vs-others position in BA completion improves with SES and is favourable to Black students only at the top of
the socioeconomic distribution — practically, only in the highest SES quintile (SES >= 0.543, top 20%, mean SES 1.09), and
there only as parity, not as a demonstrable advantage.**

1. **Unadjusted rates (framing i).** Black completion is *below* the comparator at every SES level (overall 1.33% vs 3.12%).
   The gap is near-closed only in the top quintile: Black 3.45% (7/203) vs non-Black 3.54% (55/1552) — rate ratio 0.97
   (90% CI 0.51–1.86), gap −0.10 pp (Newcombe 90% CI −1.88 to +2.78), Fisher p = 1.00, bootstrap P(gap>0) = 0.46.
   Against White-only the picture is identical (3.45% vs 3.53%, ratio 0.98). Lower quintiles show 2.5–4× relative
   deficits (ratios 0.21, 0.24, 0.65, 0.32). Note the narrowing is **not monotone in percentage-point terms** (Q4 is the
   second-worst bin, −2.64 pp); it is a top-quintile phenomenon, not a smooth dose-response (Spearman rho = 0.30, p = 0.62).
   Q1 looks "narrow" (−0.70 pp) only because of a floor effect (0.19% vs 0.89% on 1 Black event).

2. **SES gradient (framing ii) — the best-supported signal.** Black completion rises much faster with SES: OR per 1 SD
   = 2.09 (90% CI 1.58–2.76) vs 1.30 (1.17–1.45) for non-Black and 1.18 (1.04–1.33) for White; top-to-bottom quintile rate
   ratio 18× (Black) vs 4× (non-Black). Black × SES interaction OR 1.61 (90% CI 1.19–2.17, p = 0.009 Firth; MLE LRT
   p = 0.015), stable across specifications (raw SES units, centring, adjustment; leave-one-Black-respondent-out beta
   0.395–0.517, never sign-flipping) and across comparators (Black-vs-White-only subset: OR 1.77, p = 0.002). The model-free
   Black × quintile-dummy test is only suggestive (p = 0.12), i.e. power, not the pattern, is the limit.

3. **Conditional on academic preparation (framing iii) — where an outright advantage appears at all.** Adding ASVAB,
   class rank and sex flips the Black coefficient from OR 0.42 (0.30–0.59) to 1.19 (0.83–1.70, n.s.), and within the top
   quintile the adjusted Black OR = 2.14 (90% CI 1.07–4.29, p = 0.071); the model-implied parity point moves from
   SES = +1.25 (unadjusted; above only 5.8% of the sample) to SES = −0.14 (adjusted; above 53.5%). This divergence between
   unadjusted and adjusted results is itself the substantive finding: the raw shortfall is carried almost entirely by
   measured preparation, and conditional on equal preparation Black students do at least as well as peers **from about the
   SES median upward, most clearly in the top quintile**. ASVAB/class rank are plausible *mediators* of a SES→completion
   path (corr with SES 0.46/0.30), so this is a conditional, not causal, contrast.

**Bottom line.** At no SES level do Black respondents significantly *exceed* non-Black or White completion rates; the
advantage the question presupposes exists in this dataset only (a) as a much steeper SES gradient, and (b) as parity-to-
slight-advantage in the **top SES quintile (SES >= 0.54, and descriptively above SES = 1.0, the top ~9%)**, where the raw
gap vanishes (3.45% vs 3.54%) and the preparation-adjusted odds favour Black students (OR ≈ 2.1, p ≈ 0.07). Because that
conclusion rests on 203 Black respondents with 7 completions (minimum detectable difference 3.9 pp; a single dropped case
moves the gap from −0.10 to −0.57 pp), it should be phrased as "the gap closes / approaches parity at the highest SES
levels", not as an established Black advantage.
